CAT = "pknlePharm"; SEG = "PHA"
Q = [
("A farmer from southern Punjab is brought in with pinpoint pupils, salivation, sweating and bradycardia after spraying crops. What is the first antidote?",
 "Atropine", ["Naloxone", "Flumazenil", "Physostigmine"],
 "This is organophosphate poisoning, a cholinergic crisis. Atropine is given in doubling doses until the chest is clear and secretions dry; pralidoxime may be added. Naloxone reverses opioids only."),
("A patient on warfarin starts a new drug and her INR climbs sharply. Which drug most likely caused it?",
 "Metronidazole", ["Rifampicin", "Carbamazepine", "Phenobarbital"],
 "Metronidazole inhibits the metabolism of warfarin, so the INR rises and bleeding risk increases. Rifampicin, carbamazepine and phenobarbital induce liver enzymes and lower the INR."),
("A man on rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide and ethambutol complains of red-green colour confusion. Which drug is responsible?",
 "Ethambutol", ["Isoniazid", "Rifampicin", "Pyrazinamide"],
 "Ethambutol causes optic neuritis, first seen as loss of red-green colour vision. Acuity and colour vision are checked before and during treatment."),
("Which first-line TB drug commonly causes peripheral neuropathy that pyridoxine prevents?",
 "Isoniazid", ["Ethambutol", "Rifampicin", "Streptomycin"],
 "Isoniazid depletes pyridoxine (vitamin B6), which causes peripheral neuropathy, especially in malnourished, diabetic or alcohol-dependent patients."),
('A man took a large paracetamol overdose. Which metabolite damages his liver?',
 'NAPQI', ['Paracetamol glucuronide', 'Paracetamol sulphate', 'Cysteine conjugate'],
 'In overdose the glucuronide and sulphate pathways saturate, so more paracetamol becomes NAPQI. When glutathione runs out NAPQI damages hepatocytes; N-acetylcysteine restores glutathione.'),
("A hypertensive patient on lisinopril develops a persistent dry cough. What is the usual mechanism?",
 "Accumulation of bradykinin", ["Excess angiotensin II", "Raised serum potassium", "Beta-2 receptor block"],
 "ACE also breaks down bradykinin, so ACE inhibitors let it build up in the airway. Switching to an angiotensin receptor blocker usually stops the cough."),
("Which antihypertensive should be avoided in early pregnancy because it harms fetal kidneys?",
 "Enalapril", ["Methyldopa", "Labetalol", "Nifedipine"],
 "ACE inhibitors and ARBs cause fetal renal failure, oligohydramnios and skull defects. Methyldopa, labetalol and nifedipine are used for hypertension in pregnancy."),
("A patient on lithium becomes toxic after starting a new drug for high blood pressure. Which drug is the likely cause?",
 "Indapamide", ["Amlodipine", "Atenolol", "Doxazosin"],
 "Thiazide and thiazide-like diuretics such as indapamide reduce lithium clearance by increasing its reabsorption in the proximal tubule, so levels rise. ACE inhibitors and NSAIDs do the same."),
]
